course: biology
lesson: 357
title: Bones by name: the skull, jaw, spine and ribs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The skull looks like one big bone, but it is 22 bones fitted together with zigzag seams called sutures, as tight as the pieces of a hard jigsaw.
explain: Only one skull bone moves: the lower jaw, which swings on a hinge just in front of each ear; put your fingers there and open your mouth to feel it.
explain: Run your hand down a friend's back and you feel a row of bumps: these are the back edges of the vertebrae, the bones of the spine, stacked in a gentle S-curve with soft cushions called discs between them.
explain: Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage, guarding the heart and lungs while still letting the chest swell when you breathe.
fact: The skull has 22 bones: 8 around the brain and 14 in the face, joined by zigzag seams called sutures.
fact: In a baby the sutures are still open, with soft spots between the bones, so the head can squeeze through at birth and the brain can grow; they close slowly into adulthood.
fact: The lower jaw, or mandible, is the strongest bone of the face and the only skull bone that moves; its hinge is just in front of the ear.
fact: The hyoid is a small horseshoe-shaped bone in the throat that is joined to no other bone; it is held by muscles and helps you swallow and speak.
fact: The spine is made of 33 vertebrae: 7 in the neck, 12 in the chest, 5 in the lower back, and the rest fused together into the base of the spine and the tailbone.
fact: Humans and giraffes both have seven neck vertebrae; a giraffe's are each very long, and almost all mammals have seven.
fact: The spine has an S-shaped curve that works like a spring and balances the head over the hips, and soft discs between the vertebrae cushion each step.
fact: The tailbone, or coccyx, is a few tiny vertebrae fused together, a leftover of the tails of our ancestors; muscles still attach to it, so a fall on it hurts.
fact: People have 12 pairs of ribs, 24 in all, and men and women have the same number; the idea that men have one rib fewer is a myth.
fact: Ribs protect the heart and lungs, and the lowest two pairs are floating ribs, not joined at the front, which leaves room to breathe and bend.
fact: A few people have an extra small rib in the neck.
exercise: How many bones make up the skull? | 22 | 1 | 8 | 206
exercise: What are the zigzag seams between skull bones called? | Sutures | Soft discs | Little hinges | Vertebrae
exercise: Why are a baby's skull bones not yet joined tight? | So the head can squeeze through at birth and the brain can grow | Because babies are born with no skull at all, only soft skin over the brain | So the baby can hear better | Because the bones are made of jelly
exercise: Which is the only skull bone that moves? | The lower jaw | The forehead bone | The cheekbone on each side | The bone of the nose
exercise: Where is the hinge of your jaw? | Just in front of the ear | Right under the middle of the chin | Behind the eyes | At the top of the neck
exercise: What is unusual about the hyoid bone in the throat? | It is joined to no other bone | It is the biggest bone in the body | It is made of soft cartilage only | It only grows once you are grown up
exercise: How many vertebrae are in the human neck? | 7 | 12 | 5 | 33
exercise: How many ribs does a person have in all? | 24 | 12 | 20 | 32
exercise: How many neck vertebrae does a giraffe have? | 7, the same as us, but very long | 70, very small ones | 25, more than any mammal | 1, a single very long bone
exercise: What are the bumps you can feel down someone's back? | The back edges of the vertebrae | The ribs sticking out through the skin of the back | The tops of the lungs | Knots in the back muscles
exercise: Why is the spine S-shaped instead of straight? | The curve works like a spring and keeps you balanced | The spine gets bent from years of sitting at school desks | The curve is only there in old people | So the ribs have room to hang
exercise: What cushions the vertebrae from each other? | Soft discs | Pockets of air | Thin layers of fat | Extra small ribs
exercise: What is the tailbone? | A few tiny fused vertebrae, a leftover of a tail | A spare bone that has no use at all and no muscles on it | Part of the hip bone | A bone that only boys have
exercise: Do men have fewer ribs than women? | No, both have 24 | Yes, men have only 23 | Yes, men have just 22 | No, men have more
exercise: Which organs do the ribs guard? | The heart and lungs | The stomach and brain | The kidneys and eyes | The liver and bladder only
exercise: What is special about floating ribs? | They are not joined at the front | They move separately from the spine | They float in the water inside the chest | They are only ever found in babies
test: «my uncle says men have one less rib cos of an old story, true?» What is right? | No, men and women both have 24 ribs | Yes, men have 23 ribs | Yes, but only some men | No, women have one less
test: Count them: a skull has 22 bones. A museum shows three human skulls. How many skull bones in all? | 66 | 25 | 22 | 44
test: Count them: a person has 12 pairs of ribs and a mouse has 13 pairs. How many ribs does the person have? | 24 | 12 | 13 | 26
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom stretches his neck long like a giraffe's, then snaps it back short. What is true about real necks? | A giraffe and a person both have seven neck bones; a giraffe's are just much longer | A giraffe's neck has hundreds of tiny bones | Necks can stretch like rubber | A person's neck has no bones at all
test: What animal has a very short neck but still has seven neck bones? | A mouse | A snake | A bird | A fish
test: «I can wiggle my whole skull, look» What is really moving? | Only the lower jaw; the other skull bones are joined tight | All 22 skull bones, sliding about on their zigzag seams | The top of the skull | The nose bone
test: What would happen if a baby's skull bones were joined tight at birth? | The head could not squeeze through so easily and the brain would have no room to grow | The baby would be much cleverer | Nothing would change | The baby's teeth would come through earlier
test: «fell on me bum, now it hurts to sit, but there's no bone there right» What is right? | The tailbone is there, and muscles attach to it | Right, there is no bone there at all, only soft fat | Only the hip bones are there | It is a muscle, not a bone, that hurts
test: In a cartoon a dog swallows a bone and his neck moves; a boy feels his own throat move when he swallows. What bone helps him swallow? | The hyoid, a small horseshoe bone held by muscles | A neck vertebra | The lower jaw | A rib
test: Why might a completely straight spine be worse than an S-shaped one? | It could not spring and cushion your steps so well | It would make you much taller, so you would bump your head | It would protect the lungs less | It would stop the ribs moving
test: Why do floating ribs help you bend sideways and breathe? | They are not joined in front, so the lower chest can move | They are made of rubber | They are joined to the hips | They float loose inside the lungs
exam: State the number of bones of which the human skull is composed. | Twenty-two | One | Eight | Thirty-three
exam: Identify the sole bone of the skull which is capable of movement. | The mandible, or lower jaw | The frontal bone of the forehead | The bone of the nose | The cheekbone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of ribs in men and in women. | Men and women alike possess twelve pairs | Men possess one rib fewer than women | Women possess one pair fewer than men | The number varies with the stature of the person
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cervical vertebrae of the giraffe? | They number seven, as in man, each being greatly elongated | They number in excess of seventy | They are fused into a single bone | They are absent, the neck being supported by muscle alone
